  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nCut-off low continues to circulate in northern Mexico near west TX \nwith low-amplitude ridging covering most of the rest of the \nsouthwestern and central US. We remain in weak flow as a result. A \nweak sfc trough has slowly passed southward through the area today \nand a small ridge of high pressure sits across western KS and \ncentral NE. Plentiful sunshine, deeper mixing, and increasing mid-\nlevel heights are all helping contribute to a warmer day today with \nall locations warming up nicely into the 60s. \n\nThe sfc ridge looks to push south this evening and set up over \neastern KS and into MO and OK overnight. This sets the stage for a \nbetter radiational fog scenario than we've seen the last two days, \nas winds look to turn calm with skies remaining clear into tonight. \nAdditionally, crossover temperatures in the mid to upper 40s are \nlikely to be reached. Morning lows are forecast in the low 40s, and \ncould still end up on the cooler side of guidance given this setup. \nThe uncertainty in temperatures is the main factor leading to \nquestions about how dense fog may become. While fog development \nlooks probable across a larger area, widespread dense fog is less \ncertain, especially toward western zones. The REFS shows probability \nof visibility under a quarter mile around 50-70% across much of \neastern KS, but higher visibilities (like 5 miles) are close to the \nsame. \n\nOnce any fog burns off Monday morning, another warm and sunny day is \nexpected with highs reaching the 70s. The mid-level ridge looks to \nflatten into Tuesday as a shortwave ejects across the Northern \nRockies, although temperatures are still forecast to reach the low \n70s in east central KS areas, mid to upper 60s otherwise. \n\nThe aforementioned shortwave looks to induce a sfc low that is \nprogged to move along the MO River Valley into Wednesday morning, \nbringing a cold front along with it. This results in breezy winds \nwith slightly cooler temperatures in the 60s, although the deeper \ncooler air lags far enough behind to hold off the stronger CAA until \nThursday. The troughing pattern deepens Thursday to our east with \nmultiple shortwaves moving through it, while an amplified ridge \ndevelops to our west. Guidance appears to be coming into better \nagreement on the westward extent of the trough, although GEFS \nmembers are still further west than others. It's enough for the \nensemble guidance to hone in on a cooler trend with higher \nconfidence in temperatures staying in the 50s Thursday and Friday. \nThe greater uncertainty at this point is how soon the trough moves \neast and allows the ridge to move overhead. That will impact how \nquickly we warm back up, which looks to occur either Saturday or \nSunday of next weekend. Still no meaningful rain chances are in the \nforecast for the next 7 days.",
